"Red" is an adjective; nonetheless, we do say "The leaf is red." An
adjective can be a predicate complement. Therefore, the reason why we
generally don't say, "The leaf is its," lies somewhere else.

My guess is that it's because we have little occasion to say such a
thing (in what context would it come up?), and therefore it sounds
funny. Arguments over whether it's "correct" or not are beside the
point: descriptive grammar precedes prescriptive grammar.

